course: dictionary
lesson: 1456
title: Working out a new word: cask
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word from the words around it. This is its context. In the sentence «they hove the wine-casks overboard, tied one to the other by a long line», you can see the casks were thrown into the sea and tied together, so they must be large things that float.
explain: The next clue is «the long bobbing procession of casks». Bobbing means moving up and down on the water, so the casks float. Big, wooden, floating containers for wine: that is a cask.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. «Wine-barrels tied in a long line» still makes sense, so a cask is a large wooden barrel. A dictionary confirms it: a cask is a large wooden barrel, made of strips of wood held by metal hoops.
fact: A cask is a large wooden barrel, made of strips of wood held together by metal hoops.
fact: Casks hold liquids such as wine, beer, water or rum.
fact: «Hove» means threw, as in «they hove the wine-casks overboard».
fact: A cooper is a craftsperson who makes casks.
fact: Porpoises are sea animals, and the casks bobbing in a line looked like «a mile of porpoises».
exercise: «They hove the wine-casks overboard.» What does hove mean? | *Threw | Sang | Rowed | Tied
exercise: What is a cask? | A type of ship's sail | A kind of seaside rock | *A large wooden barrel for holding liquids | A small glass bottle
exercise: Which word from the sentences means a sea animal? | casks | *porpoises | shellfish | olive-woods
test: What were the wine-casks tied together with? | *A long line | A sail | A metal hoop | A fishing net
test: Which job makes casks? | A porpoise | *A cooper | A sailor | A pirate
exam: «Like a mile of porpoises» suggests the casks were | Small and light, like toys | *Many casks bobbing in a long line on the water | Hidden under the sea | Sleeping on the sand
